## Introduction
Biological entities, e.g. proteins, DNA, cells, bacteria, viruses, and their derivative biosystems are indisputably the most important biological resources in life, and have aroused great attentions in fundamental research, pathological diagnosis and biotechnological applications [1]. However, such practical achievements are regrettably restricted by their fragile nature, such as poor operative stability, narrow optimum pH range, susceptible to organic solvents or pressures, and others [2]. Cryopreservation, whereby biosamples are frozen at lower temperature for biopreservation and biobanking, can effectively prevent the structural degradation and denaturation to a certain extent, and meanwhile can keep their intrinsic biological functionalities during collection, processing, transport or storage [3]. However, some new troubles began to appear, such as damage from repeated freeze-thaw cycles, specialized facilities/space/equipment, extra budget for "cold chain" logistics and so on. Therefore, it will greatly improve the veracity and reliability of bioterials to develop a new technique for preservation with no need of "cold-chain", which might open up a new avenue for industrial and/or biomedical applications.
Researchers have drawn their inspiration from nature's wisdom by studying how biospecies survive in extreme conditions. As we know, Maryna umbrellata [4], Bacillus subtilis [5], as well as Tardigrades [6] can form a natural robust, solid shell to protect their "soft bodies" in response to environmental stresses [7]. These intriguing features in nature have inspired researchers to preserve the fragile biological entities by chemically forming protective coating on the surface [8–10]. Some artificial protective materials like silica [11,12], calcium phosphate [9,13], graphene [14], polydopamine [15], chitosan [16], silk fibroin [17,18] and others [19–21] have been employed for the protection of various kind of biological entities such as proteins [22], oligonucleotides [23], viruses [24], microbes [25] and mammalian cells [26], whereby it can enhance the resistance against many kind of environmental stressors [27]. Although great success has been made, such artificial coatings are quite easy to induce the irreversible denaturation and unexpected deactivation of encapsulated biological entities. In addition, the degradability of these shells is also crucial and unquestionably required for absolute recovery of their intrinsic biological functionalities. Therefore, the development of a universal technique that can be widely employed for preservation of multitudinous biological entities is absolutely necessary to improve their own structural stability and biological performance.
Metal–organic framework (MOF), built from metal nodes and organic ligands through metal-coordination bonds, is an emerging class of inorganic crystalline porous materials [28–30]. Since it was termed in 1995, MOF has been exploited for a wide variety of applications, including gas capture and separations [31,32], energy storage and conversion [33,34], chemical/biological sensors [35,36], disease diagnose and therapy [37,38], and many others [39–41]. The design of MOFs with varied architectures and chemical functionality has also underpinned studies describing the interface of MOF and biology and even developing advanced MOF biocomposites. As a new emerging topic, the research at the bio-MOF interfaces would produce great breakthroughs in both fundamental science and revolutionary biotechnologies, through adsorption, covalent conjugation, infiltration or encapsulation of various biological entities with MOF crystals [42].
After nearly ten years of continuous exploration, MOF have exhibited their unprecedented physical properties for the encapsulation and preservation of different bioentities due to the following obvious advantages: (1) The finely tunable porosity and chemistry of MOF allow the specific bioentities to be embeded into the porous frameworks with high loading capacity [43,44], meanwhile the open space inside facilitates the diffusion of small molecules
like substrates, nutrients, etc.; (2) The robust frameworks endow the encapsulated bioentities to defense against different harsh attacks such as heat [45], mediums [46], pressure [47], irradiation [48], proteases [49] and others [50,51]; (3) The appeared weak supramolecular interactions between MOF skeletons and bioentities could prevent the leaching of encapsulated bioentities, meanwhile it makes reliable to completely release of individual bioentities after MOFs being rapidly disintegrated under specified conditions.
MOFs have been employed as universal biomimetic exoskeletons for the encapsulation of a large variety of biological entities ranging from peptides, enzymes, the repeutic proteins and nucleic acids, DNA, viruses, living cells even to biological devices (Fig. 1) [52,53]. Furthermore, these MOF encapsulated bioentity (bioentity@MOF) composites exhibit strong inflexibility and excellent biological activities, which can be used in constructing advanced biosystems for biological and biomedical applications [54,55]. Despite being in the embryonic stage, MOFs have not only achieved great success for the encapsulation of different bioentities, but also exhibited excellent preservation efficiency and great potential for development of advanced bio-relevant materials. The past ten years have witnessed a rapidly increasing amount of publications in relation to the design and application of enzyme/MOF biohybrids. Although few review articles have described the recent progress on MOFs for enzyme immobilization and bioapplications [42,56–61], but a systematic overview for acknowledging the MOF bioencapsulation of bioentities with whole spectrum is still missing up to now.
Herein, we comprehensively present the recent achievements about the MOF encapsulation for the preservation of numerous kind of bioentities including biopharmaceuticals, enzymes, biofluids, viruses, living organisms and even biological devices. This field has experienced a recent surge mostly in designing enzymatic nanofactories (single enzyme reactions, cascade reactions), controlled delivery of biological species (therapeutic proteins and nucleic acids, biofluids) as well as the protection of organisms (viruses, bacteria, yeasts) and biodevices (fingerprints, diagnostic biochips). The preservation of various type of bioentities is classified into three sections in this review: biomacromolecules, viruses and living organisms, and biological devices. We are collating herein most of the MOFs that have been utilized for biopreservation and listed in Table 1 along with their constituting ligands, metal nodes and code name MOFs. We hope this review would facilitate the development of general protocols of MOFs for bioencapsulation and biopreservation, and also deliver future perspectives on the construction of robust biomaterials for applications in the field of industrial productions or modern biomedical technologies.
## Encapsulation strategies
MOF itself intrinsically possesses high surface area, controllable structure as well as tunable pore size which endows with the loading capability of different cargoes, especially for gases [62–64], molecular probes [65,66], small nanoparticles [67], therapeutic drugs [68,69] and so on [70,71]. Cargoes can be generally encapsulated into porous structures through diffusing into the pre-existing MOF pores directly (post-synthetic infiltration) or one-pot embedding bioentities during the process of MOF synthesis (in situ encapsulation). However, because of the fragile nature and large-scale structure of bioentities, their encapsulation into the MOF is often restricted by the following constraints: postsynthetic infiltration technique could be used for small bioentities with dimension analogous to the MOF porous apertures [72–74], while in situ encapsulation approach only for those whom MOF crystalline can happen around their surface with no side effect. In
