## Post-synthetic infiltration
Post-synthetic infiltration is a process in which bioentity is diffused into the porous structure of MOF driven by the concentration gradient. In this process, individual MOF crystals are
pre-synthesized and then co-incubated with bioentities under biocompatible condition. Clearly, the infiltration of bioentity into MOF structures can happen only if its steric structure fits well with the pore gemetory [57], meanwhile MOF structures are able to be stably existed under encapsulation conditions [75–77]. In MOF structures, organic ligands usually serve as edge of pore structures to connect each metal node spatially, which further determines the pore-opening of MOF and encapsulable structures of bioentities [78–80]. Long organic linkers are usually required to synthesize the MOFs with ultrahigh porosity, which might provide larger void space for bioentity encapsulation. The available MOF frameworks generally afford two different type of structures: One is consisting of polyhy
dral cages that are larger than the aperture (pore-opening window), which term cage-type MOF; Another is term channel-type MOF that containing pores with dimension equal to that of aperture.
Numerous attempts have been made to develop mesoporous MOFs (mesoMOFs) in order to improve the loading capability and endow their specific functions. The pore size of these structures match well with some of enzymes, therapeutic proteins as well as oligonucleotides. Also, the multiple supramolecular interactions between bioentity and framework ligands including hydrophilic interfacial interactions, \(\pi\cdots\pi\) interactions, electrostatic interactions, etc. can facilitate the rapid infiltration, meanwhile prevent the leaching of encapsulated bioentities. Sometimes, the bioentities can also undergo the conformation change to reach the maximum interactions in stereoscopic space, which greatly improve the encapsulation efficiency, diminish self-aggregation, eliminate leaching and optimize microenvironment. Up to now, some mesoMOFs including NU-100x series, PCN-series, HKUST-1, MIL-101, UiO-66, etc. (Table 2) have been employed successfully as encapsulants to wrap and preserve different kind of biomacromolecules, such as MP-11, Cyt c, Mb, HRP, CAT, SOD, insulin, tyrosinase, lipase, nucleotide, and many others [45,46,54,79,81–102].
Because the crystallization process of MOFs is independent with the infiltration of bioentities, MOFs can be synthesized under solvothermal/hydrothermal condition with highly crystalline structures, low defects and excellent aqueous stabilities [103,104], which greatly expanded the appliable structures in MOF bank. The developed bioentity@MOF composites show excellent resistance against external mechanical pressure, chemical/biological corrosion and physical irradiation. In addition, some MOF structures are able to be decomposed when triggered by external stimuli including pH, EDTA, phosphate buffer and so on [99,105], an effective way for preservation and controlled release of biospecimen and bio-based therapeutics in vitro and in vivo. This strategy has been extensively employed for encapsulation of enzymes or biopharmatericals in development of enzymic nanoreactors and drugs delivery platforms.
However, restricted by the pore window and infiltration mechanism, this strategy can be only employed for encapsulating small bioentities like peptides, oligonucleotides and functional proteins with low molecular weight, which limited the potential application of bio@MOF composites. For more complex bioentity systems like cell-free expression systems, microorganisms, animal/plant cells as well as more sophisticated tissue samples, it is far beyond the power since the competitive infiltration and small porous structures. In addition, the MOF ligands in this strategy are usually polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons for supporting the framework structures, it's getting more worse for biological application in vivo. Therefore, it is urgently needed to develop another one-pot approach that can encapsulate bioentities that irrelevant to their molecular dimensions.
## In situ encapsulation
Different from the post-synthetic infiltration, the in situ encapsulation is an approach to combining the metal nodes and organic linkers of frameworks together with target bioentity, and forming the framework structures with bioentity in situ encapsulated inside [49,106,107]. Considering their fragile nature of most bioentities, MOF (e.g. ZIF-series, HKUST) should be synthesized under mild conditions so as to retain their original structures and functions. It has shown that the surface chemistry of bioentity influences the nucleation rates of MOF precursors, which might further determines their final efficiency of encapsulation and preservation [108]. From the perspective of growth mechanism, the in situ encapsulation of bioentity generally happens through the following two competing strategies [61,109]. The first approach is that the nucleation of
MOF precursors is facilitated by the bioentity surface and further grow into bioentity@MOF crystals, which is termed as "biomimetic mineralization". Another is termed as "co-precipitation" process, where biological species are passively coprecipitated with the MOF crystallization. In this process, capping agents (i.e. polyvinylpyrrolidone, PVP) are usually needed to stabilize the bioentities and to aid the nucleation of MOF precursors.
The in situ encapsulation is happened by growing MOF crystals surrounding the bioentity interfaces, which is not influenced by its dimension and structures. Since MOF structures are nucleated and growd on the surface of bioentities, the interfacial chemistry including hydrophobicity, electronegativity as well as sniuthness can effect the growth behavior and encapsulation capability. Researchers are able to reconstruct the surface of bioentity by simple chemical modification to reach the efficient encapsulation and preservation of bioentities with a broad spectrum. Recent advances in one-pot synthesis of MOF on bioentities (Table 3) have allowed them as coatings for protection of biomacromolecules, organisms and even biological devices. These bioentities can be not only encapsulated into the frameworks effectively, but also improved their defence ability against different extreme environmental conditions, such as organic solvent, elevated temperature, UV irradiation, proteolytic enzyme, acid/alkaline, starvation and others [43,48–53,106,109–144].
Since this strategy is independent with the bioentity structures, it is theoretically able to encapsulate all kind of bioentities no matter the dimension of bioentity is larger or not. It might be appliable for preservation of complex biosystems like living organisms and micro/nano-sized biodevices. However, in this strategy, MOF precursors are co-existed with bioentities, they might induce the unexpected deactivation in forming MOF structures. Besides, water-soluable, bio-friendly ligands are usually needed in order to avoid the denaturation and inactivation of bioentities. Meanwhile, this strategy could generally show low encapsulation efficiency, poor structural stability, high crystalline defects as well as uncertain bioentity distribution. ZIF-series (e.g. ZIF-8, ZIF-10, ZIF-90, etc.) are the commonly used MOF platforms for in situ encapsulation due to their aqueous-phase synthesizable, excellent biocompatibility and controlled degradation [145,146]. And also, some others MOF matrices like HKUST-1, MAF-7, Eu/Tb-BDC, etc. have also been reported [52].
## Preservation of biomacromolecules
Biomacromolecules like proteins as well as oligonucleotides have become the indispensable biosources to most advanced biological science and technology [147]. However, such practical applications are hindered by their fragile nature, such as low thermal stability, narrow optimum pH range, low tolerance to organic solvents and hydrolases, etc. [148]. MOF encapsulation can bring the following advantages: structural stability, biological functionalities as well as long-term storage. In addition, the enzyme@MOF hybrids can further allow the access of small substrates to pass through the narrow channel, which can be employed to develop enzymatic nanofactories for the application in the fields of enzyme engineering and industrial biocatalysis. Besides, the development of biodisposable MOF structures may open up a new avenue for the encapsulation and controlled release of protein drugs or geneic sequences for diagnosis and treatment of diseases.
## Enzymatic nanofactories
Enzymes are indisputably one of the most important biomacromolecules in life for their great applications in pharmaceuticals, fuel production, food processing as well as life-sustaining biolog-
ical transformations [149,150]. The MOF-encapsulated enzymes can provide the following advantages except for the conventional items: (1) The three-dimensional structural confinement within porous MOFs endows enzyme with excellent functionality and stable spatial configuration, which are able to prevent both aggregation and leaching of enzymes [85]; (2) Uniform pore size of MOF structures can be designed to control the access of solvents, substrates and products accurately, which guarantees the overall catalytic efficiency and the selective catalysis [84]; (3) MOFs can be used as general supports for encapsulating various enzymes because of their ultra-large cavities, especially applicable for sophisticated multienzyme systems [121]. Typically, MOF-based enzymatic nanofactories are divided into three categories in this review according to the construction strategy and the system complexity: single-enzyme infiltration, single-enzyme encapsulation in situ, and multi-enzyme cascades.
## Single-enzyme infiltration
Cage-type MOF. Cage-type MOF is the one that contains cage-type pores that are larger than the aperture (window) through which the pore is accessible. It has recently been employed for the infiltration of biomacromolecules including enzymes, therapeutic proteins, and others through the rational designed structures and properties [151]. In some cases, biomacromolecules must undergo the conformational change to squeeze through the apertures, which might cause the irreversibly deactivation since affecting their active sites. In 2011, Ma's group firstly reported a pioneering work to immobilize MP-11 into terbium-based mesoMOF (Tb-mesoMOF) crystals, which laid a groundwork for the futural development of enzyme nanoreactors [81]. Tb-mesoMOF was composed of two mesoscopic cages (3.9 nm and 4.7 nm in diameter) with nanoscopic aperture of
about 1.3 nm and 1.7 nm respectively. It provided adequate spaces for accommodation of small enzymes like MP-11 (1.1 nm × 1.7 nm × 3.3 nm), Cyt c (2.6 nm × 3.2 nm × 3.3 nm) or Mb (2.1 nm × 3.5 nm × 4.4 nm) by entering through the mesopores [81–84]. Take Cyt c as research model, they found Cyt c could successfully access into the interior of Tb-mesoMOF despite the larger molecular dimension relative to the access pore window. The mechanism studies demonstrated enzymes underwent a significant conformation change during translocation into the MOF interior: partial perturbation with a conformation amenable to migration upon binding to surface pore; after entering into the small nanopores, enzyme was then stabilized through the exposure of hydrophobic amino acids to the hydrophobic interior of the MOF cages (Fig. 2a) [83]. Also, the strong π···π interactions between enzymes and MOF structures were able to prevent the leaching of infiltrated enzymes from mesoMOF structures, which laid the foundation for developing enzyme nanoreactors with improved stability and activity [82].
