Inspired by such achievements, Zhou et al. developed a series of stable PCN-332 and PCN-333 with rationally designed mesoporous cages for construction of enzymatic nanofactories [85]. PCN-333 (Al) featured a \(F d{-}3\;\;m\) topology with three different
sions, which exhibited quite different catalytic efficiency. HRP and Cyt c were most likely accessed into the cage in a single-in-one approach because of their comparable size with MOF cages, which smaller MP-11 might be infiltrated in a multi-in-one approach. Enzyme activity assays indicated that the immobilized HRP and Cyt c showed smaller \( K_{m} \) than free enzymes meanwhile maintaining comparable catalytic efficiency, but the catalytic activity of smaller MP-11 decreased significantly after immobilization, which indicated the MOF immobilized enzymes with compatible structures could effectively eliminate enzyme aggregation and leaching. Meanwhile, the immobilized enzymes showed excellent recyclability and high catalytic efficiency. Their catalytic activities remained almost unchanged after several cycles or even in organic solvents.
In addition, this approach was further employed for preservation of the disease-related enzymes for various biomedical applications. The same group constructed TYR@PCN-333 nanoreactor by immobilizing TYR enzyme \((5.5\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,5.5\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,5.6\ \mathrm{nm})\) into PCN-333 mesocages by activating the nontoxic prodrug APAP to cytotoxic AOBQ for cancer therapy (Fig. 2c) [86]. It was found that the TYR could accommodate into hexacaidecahedron cage with loading capability about \(0.8\mathrm{~g~g^{-1}}\) . The TYR@NPCN-333 biocom
posites not only exhibited comparable catalytic efficacy to that of free TYR, but also improved the resistance towards degradation in biological medium, such as trypsin or acidic medium. It was found that TYR@NPCN-333 could cellularly internalized into cancer cells (SKOV3-TR, H1299 or HeLa) to activate the prodrug APAP and displayed a concentration-correlated viability. The cytotoxicity of TYR@NPCN-333–APAP biohybrids to cancer cells came from the induced oxidative stress, as manifested by a combination of ROS induction and GSH depletion. The in vivo experiments in mice showed the tumour regression was completely eradicated after being treated with TYR@NPCN-333–APAP (Fig. 2d), which was envisioned to be a novel platform for vast biomedical and biotechnological applications.
In addition, series of cage-type MOFs have also been successfully employed for enzyme preservation using this strategy. For example, MIL-101(Cr) composing \(2.9\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(3.4\ \mathrm{nm}\) cages for immobilization of small peroxidase MP-8 \((1.1\ \mathrm{nm}\times1.7\ \mathrm{nm}\times3.3\ \mathrm{nm})\) [88]. Mesoporous cavities \((2.9\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(3.6\ \mathrm{nm})\) of MIL-101(Al)- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) were used for infiltration of protease (nearly \(3.0\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,3.7\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,5.8\ \mathrm{nm})\) [89]. ADH \((3.0\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,3.7\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,5.2\ \mathrm{nm})\) was infiltrated in PCN-333 (dodecahedron \(4.2\ \mathrm{nm}\) and hexacaidecahedron
\(5.5~\mathrm{nm}\) ) for conversion of toxic levels of alcohols in cells to aldehydes [87]. Khashab et al. designed a prepare big pore MIL-101 (BP-MIL-101) with pore size around \(16\;\mathrm{nm}\) and \(30\;\mathrm{nm}\) , which can be used for infiltration of larger enzymes like CAT \((4.4\,\mathrm{nm}\times4.9\,\mathrm{nm}\) \(\times\ 5.6\ \mathrm{nm})\) [90]. These immobilized enzymes displayed enhanced activity under extreme pH and high temperatures, and also exhibited long-term stability, recyclability and excellent compatibility with competing enzymes.
Channel-type MOF. In analogy to the cage-type MOF, channel-type MOF is the one that contains channel-type pores with dimension equal to the aperture at the two ends. It generally affords multiple polyhedral channels with dimension and geometry depending on its framework topology. The channels are accessible for biomacromolecules from the two ends, meanwhile the windows that vertically presented between the channels allow the samll substrates to access the immobilized biomacromolecules, which might be an excellent platform for biological nanofactories. Zirconium-based MOFs like NU-100x series, always feature an extended csq-net topology with mesoporous hexagonal and microporous triangular channels, which potentially appliable for enzyme infiltration. Farha and co-workers have made great progress towards design rules for enzyme immobilization in channel-type mesoMOFs (Fig. 3a). They found that enzymes could access into the mesoporous channel if the minimum cross-sectional dimension matched well with the channel structures. In this case, the change of enzyme conformation that cause the permanent deactivation didn't have to happen. In situ CLSM indicated that enzymes could be immobilized into MOF channels by one-dimensional diffusion from the two ends towards the centre, and the enzyme activity showed significant dependency to the size of MOF crystals [91]. Because of beneficial confinement effect and eliminated unrecognized residual aggregation, the initial hydrolysis rate of infiltrated OPAA (4.4 \(\mathrm{nm}\times4.4\:\mathrm{nm}\times7.8\:\mathrm{nm})\) in short NU-1003 rods (OPAA@NU-1003- \(300\,\mathrm{nm}\) ) was several-times faster than long OPAA@NU-1003-7000 nm and even free OPAA for hydrolysis of nerve agent DFP and GD (Fig. 3b,c). Such MOF carrier might be appliable for in vivo agent degradation in the future.
The activity of infiltrated enzymes can be tuned by the rational design of framework structures. Yaghi et al. further reported a strategy to expand the pore aperture of channel-type MOF structure, MOF-74, from \(1.4\,\mathrm{nm}\) to previously unattained \(9.8\,\mathrm{nm}\) through increasing the length of organic linkers [79]. Such designed MOF structures showed controllable encapsulation of cargoes from Vitamin \(\mathtt{B}_{12}\) \((2.7\;\mathrm{nm})\) , metal-organic polyhedron-18 \((3.4\;\mathrm{nm})\) , Mb (2.1 \(\mathrm{nm}\times3.5\:\mathrm{nm}\times4.4\:\mathrm{nm})\) to GFP \((3.4\:\mathrm{nm}\times3.4\:\mathrm{nm}\times4.5\:\mathrm{nm})\) . Also, this strategy could be employed for design of MOF NU-100x series with different pore aperture 2s from 3 to 7, channel size from \(4.4~\mathrm{nm}\) to \(6.7~\mathrm{nm}\) ). It was found the enzymatic activity sequence of immobilized LacDH \((4.4\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,4.4\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,5.6\ \mathrm{nm})\) versus the free LacDH in catalytic oxidation of L-lactate was in the following order: LacDH@NU-1007 \(>\) LacDH@NU-1006 \(>\) LacDH@NU-1005 \(>\) free LacDH \(>\) LacDH@NU-1004 \(>\) LacDH@NU-1003, which was quite consistent with the dimension of MOF channels [92]. The diamond window of NU-1004 \((1.4\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,1.6\ \mathrm{nm})\) and NU-1003 \((1.2\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\) \(1.3\;\mathrm{nm})\) was noticeably smaller than the size of hydrated L-lactate \((0.8\,\mathrm{nm})\) and coenzyme NAD+ \((1.7\,\mathrm{nm})\) , which limited the diffusion to immobilized LacDH and thus resulting a lower enzymic activity than free LacDH.
Enzyme infiltration into MOF have been demonstrated to afford enzymic activity and preserve structural stability, which are potential for developing enzymic devices like enzyme electrodes. The same group further infiltrated FDH \((4.0\,\mathrm{nm}\times6.0\,\mathrm{nm}\times11\,\mathrm{nm})\) into large porous NU-1006 \((6.2\ \mathrm{nm})\) for \(C0_{2}\) adsorption and catalytic conversion [93,94]. The large mesoporous structure facilitates the access of coenzyme NADH/NAD+ and \(C0_{2}\) adsorption, makes the
activity of FDH@NU-1006 comparable to free FDH in transforming \(C0_{2}\) to formic acid. After depositing FDH@NU-1006 biocomposites onto a Rh-FTO glass electrode, the electro-enzymatic reaction system could efficiently convert \(C0_{2}\) into biomass at a rate of \(79\pm3.4\) \(\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) with electrochemical regeneration of the NADH coenzyme [93]. Further, after being anchored electron-mediator Rh complex to the nodes of NU-1006 to facilitate the ultrafast photoinduced electron transfer, the FDH@Rh-NU-1006 could adsorb white light for \(C0_{2}\) reduction at a high turnover frequency of about \(865\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) in 24 h mediated by recyclable coenzyme \(\mathrm{MOD}^{+}\) (Fig. 3d,e) [94]. These results could open a new way for high-efficiency \(C0_{2}\) fixation and biomass conversion through enzymatic catalytic reactions.
Some others enzymatic nanofactories were also developed according to their actual demands by infiltrating different enzymes or employing different channel-type MOFs. For example, infiltration of OPAA \((4.4\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,4.4\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,7.8\ \mathrm{nm})\) into PCN-128y (mesopore \(4.2\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) for nerve agent hydrolysis [45]. Cutinase (3.0 \(\mathrm{nm}\,\times\,3.0\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,4.5\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) was infiltrated into different structured MOF frameworks including NU-1000 (mesopore \(3.2\;\mathrm{nm}\) ), PCN-600 (mesopore \(3.1\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) and CYCU-3 (mesopore \(2.7\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) for aliphatic esters hydrolysis [46]. PCN-128 (mesopore \(4.6\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) immobilized LacDH (LacDH@PCN-128) for L-lactate oxidation [92]. Infiltration of BSL2 \((3.5\,\mathrm{nm}\times3.6\,\mathrm{nm}\times4.2\,\mathrm{nm})\) with hierarchically porous CuBTC framework \((34\,\mathrm{nm})\) for biocatalysis of esterification [95]. These immobilized enzymes showed excellent catalytic performance and great resistance to organic solvent, high temperature, wide pH as well as denaturant, and might potential for industrial and biotechnological applications.
## Single-enzyme in situ encapsulation
Although great improvements have been made for enzymatic nanofactories via post-synthetic infiltration approach, failures still occur due to the aperture dimension-limited infiltration of enzymes. One-pot approach is able to provide a new solution because enzymes are encapsulated into the pore defects of MOF structures, irrelevance to its size. This approach can be greatly expanded to construct various enzyme@MOF biohybrids. Some research groups have reported compelling results for developing enzymatic nanofactories.
Co-precipitation. PVP-mediated co-precipitation is an approach for enzyme encapsulation in situ. Liu's and Ge's groups used this method to encapsulate Cyt c with ZIF-8 in the methanol solvent. PVP could assist the dispersion and stabilization of Cyt c in methanol solution containing MOF precursors \((Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) , Hmlm) [110]. Fluorescence spectroscopy indicated ZIF-8 highly preserved the configuration of Cyt c after being embedded in ZIF-8 compared to its native counterpart in solution (Fig. 4a). Importantly, the peroxidase activity of Cyt c@ZIF-8 composites was 10-fold higher than the free Cyt c in oxidizing ABTS with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (Fig. 4b). It might be the reason that the change of Cyt c conformation and microenvironment increased substrate affinity toward \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and meanwhile the interactions between enzyme and framework significantly enhanced the catalytic efficacy. In a representative study, the excellent peroxidase activity of Cyt c@ZIF-8 afforded a quite lower peroxide detection limit with value about \(3\ \mathrm{nm}\) at a signal-to-noise ratio of 3 in the presence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and Amplex Red substrates. Moreover, this method could be also employed for the encapsulation of different enzymes (HRP and lipase) with other MOF scaffolds (ZIF-10). Ma and Yang characterized the orientation and dynamics of the PVP-modified T4L in T4L@ZIF-8 composite, and proposed a possible orientation mechanism for the first time: The orientation was dominantly enslaved by the steric hindrance and the \(\pi\!-\!\pi\) stacking interactions between ZIF-8 imidazole rings and aromatic residues, which gave us fundamental guidance for improving MOF-
based enzyme reactor by optimizing the building blocks of MOFs [11].
Although PVP was used to stabilize enzymes, the methanol solvent can still easily make enzymes denaturation and deactivation during the MOF process. It is urgently needed to develop aqueous phase synthesizable MOF structures by enhancing the interactions between MOF and enzymes for rapid nucleation in aqueous medium. Shieh and Tsung et al. reported the first aqueous phase synthesis of CAT-immobilized ZIF-90 (CAT@ZIF-90) biocomposites via co-precipitation method from an aqueous solution containing appropriate amounts of PVP-modified CAT, zinc nitrate and 2-ICA [49]. The peroxide-decomposing activity of CAT@ZIF-90 prepared by aqueous method yielded an observed rate constant \(\left(k_{obs}\right)\) of \(0.0268\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\) in the presence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and Amplex Red substrates. Unsurprisingly, the conventional ethanol-method synthesized CAT@ZIF-90 as a comparison showed no activity because of its fragile nature [106].
The CAT@ZIF-90 composite exhibited low activity compared to free CAT under denaturant-free conditions due to the hindered substrate diffusion and/or changed enzyme orientations, but the confinement of ZIFs could shield against the structural unfolding of CAT, which allowed the biological activity of CAT@ZIF-90 being retained after incubation with protease (proteinase K, \(6.8\:\mathrm{nm}\times6.8\) \(\mathrm{nm}\,\times\,11\ \mathrm{nm}\)), urea (0.05 and \(6\ \mathrm{M}\)) or even at elevated temperature \((80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) (Fig. 4c, d). On the other side, no activity was observed
for free CAT under the similar denaturation conditions. In contrast, 3-AT \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{N}_{4}\right)\), a small molecule that can freely infiltrate into the MOF pores, was able to inhibit CAT function by forming stabilized \(\mathsf{C A T-H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}\) complex. With the presence of 3-AT, it was found that the activity of CAT@ZIF-90 composite could be fully halted, which further proved the hypothesis that the maintenance of enzyme function in enzyme@MOF biohybrids comes from the structural confinement.
Biomimetic mineralization. There is another way that don't need capping agents for stabilization. MOF can be directly formed around enzyme surface through facilitating the MOF nucleation process under physiological conditions, which termed as "biomimetic mineralization". Liang and Falcaro found a broad-spectrum biocarromolecules (including enzymes, proteins and DNA) could be in situ encapsulated into the ZIF-8 frameworks when they were mixed with precursors \((Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\), Hmlm) in aqueous solution (Fig. 5a) [52]. Remarkably, ZIF-8 encapsulated HRP (HRP@ZIF-8) could preserve more than \(85\ \%\) catalytic efficacy for conversion of pyrogallol to purpurogallin even in the presence of trypsin, or even in boiling water \((100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and boiling DMF \((153\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\), which was quite higher than other commonly used protective coatings like \(\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) (Fig. 5b). Not only that, they also found this method was also applicable for other kind of MOF frameworks including but not limited to HKUST-1, Eu/Tb-BDC as well as MIL-88A, in pre
serving a myriad of enzymes (like HRP, RNase A, lysozyme, urease, (PQQ)GDH, etc.) [52].
Ouyang et al. further reported a universal and versatile AAOPE strategy, a reformed biomimetic mineralization strategy, to encapsulate all kind of proteins into ZIF-8 nondestructively with ultrahigh loading efficiency [112]. PVP was used to enrich the Cys residues on protein surface and thus form a metallothionein-like protein to accelerate the formation of prenucleation clusters around proteins regardless of the surface chemical properties (Fig. 5c-e). The developed \(\mathrm{HRP@ZIF-8}\) enzyme nanoreactors kept the native structural conformation of enzymes due to the ZIF-8 confinement, maintained high bioactivity than \(\mathrm{HRP@CaCO}_{3}\) and free HRP under harsh conditions, including unfolding agents (trypsin and urea), elevated temperature \((100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and organic solvent (DMF at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) (Fig. 5f). Moreover, this surface-charge independent strategy could be employed for the high-efficiency encapsulation of a broad range of enzymes (e.g. MB, HRP, Cyt c, urease, GOx, RNase A, RNase B, trypsin, Pepsin), functional proteins (e.g. OVA, TRF, BSA and human serum) and other cargo (e.g. AuNP, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and CQDs) within different MOFs (ZIF-8 and HKUST-1) [112]. Such reformed biomimetic strategy has great promise for enzyme capsulation in the fields of biomedicine, catalysis, and biosensing.
After that, Doonan's and Falcaro's groups cooperated together to investigate the influence of the enzyme encapsulation methods and MOF hydrophobicity to the preservation efficacy of enzyme nanoreactors (Fig. 6a) [109]. It was found that capping agent PVP didn't play essential role in enhancing enzyme loading, but it could induce the formation of small ZIF-8 nanoparticles ( \(120\,\mathrm{nm}\) versus \(500\,\mathrm{nm}\) ) through co-precipitation method. Additionally, the encapsulated
urease through the biomimetic mineralization process showed higher enzymic activity and remained more stable at broad temperature range from room temperature to \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) than that from co-precipitation method, (Fig. 6b).
In addition, the activity of encapsulated enzyme via biomimetic mineralization showed obvious dependency on the hydrophobicity of MOF materials (Fig. 6c) [113]. It was shown that after encapsulating into three similar MOF structures including hydrophobic ZIF-8, hydrophilic MAF-7 as well as hydrophilic ZIF-90, CAT@ZIF-8 showed no measurable catalytic activity because the hydrophobic framework hindered the diffusion of substrate, whereas hydrophilic CAT@MAF-7 retained comparable activity (3.2 \(\times\ 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\)) to that of free CAT \((4.4\,\times\,10^{-3}\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1})\). For another hydrophilic CAT@ZIF-90, the activity was substantially reduced to \(1.4\,\times\,10^{-3}\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) due to the mass transport limitations (Fig. 6d). Meanwhile, MAF-7, compared to ZIF-8 and ZIF-90, could further offer superior preservation efficiency from inhospitable environments including elevated temperature \((50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\), unfolding agent (urease), proteolytic agent (protease) and even organic solvents (DMSO and THF). Not only that, Jia et al. found the morphology of MOF could also affect the final activity of encapsulated enzymes [43]. The CAT@ZIF with cruciate flower-like morphology exhibited \(400\,\%\) higher activity than that of CAT@ZIF-8 composites with conventional rhombic dodecahedral morphology.
In order to further study the factors that influencing the enzymic activity, Ouyang et al. have made great progress on ZIF-8 formation mechanisms [114]. After encapsulating six different enzyme models (GOx, Cyt C, HRP, CAT, UOx and ADH).) into ZIF-8, they found the embedding patterns could affect the final activity of encap
sulated enzyme. Enzyme@ZIF-8 could maintain high enzymatic activity that comparable to free enzymes if enzymes were encapsulated through rapid nucleation of ZIF-8, whease the activity will be lower when the encapsulation was driven by slow co-precipitation because enzymes tended to be inactive due to unfolding effects and coordination with ligands (Fig. 6e). This might be reason why the encapsulated enzymes via biomimetic mineralization showed higher enzymatic activity than that via the co-precipitation
method. Interestingly, these two embedding patterns could be easily switched only by charge inversion of protein surface, which accelerating the utilization of MOF encapsulation in sophisticated applications (Fig. 6f). After being modified to negatively charged surface with acetic anhydride, the enzymatic activities of encapsulated HRP (HRP-A) were significantly improved to \( 82.9\% \), while only \( 4.3\% \) was observed for HRP@ZIF-8 without modification (Fig. 6g,h). Liang et al. also confirmed that the carboxylate groups on the pro
tein surfaces were crucial for stabilization of MOF exoskeletons rather than the amines or hydrogen groups [116]. The stabilization effects are quite different for two different MOF structures (ZIF-8 or ZIF-L) although they had same type of coordination bonds and organic linkers. Meanwhile, the stability of MOF structures and enzymes were both improved even under extreme conditions after enzyme facilitated the formation of MOF structures.
All these results have proved that in situ encapsulation method might be an universal way to develop numerous enzyme nanoreactors, which could be further utilized for application in biotechnologies and biomedines. Liang et al. reported a submarine-like biocatalytic micromotor with pH-triggered buoyancy control through co-encapsulation of CAT and pH-responsive polymer PDPA
(CAT/PDPA@ZIF-L) [117]. With the aid of hydrophilic/hydrophobic PDPA, \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generated from CAT/PDPA@ZIF-L micromotor utilizing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) as chemical fuel were able to bind with the hydrophobic PDPA at neutral pH (7.4), leading to the rapid floating motion. In contrast, at slight acidic pH (6.3), \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)will competitively bind at the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) site of the hydrophilic PDPA, causing the descending motion of the micromotors. Importantly, the anti-cancer drug (DOX) loaded CAT/PDPA@ZIF-L micromotor showed directional cytotoxicity to three-dimensional cell cultures due to the low pH of the cellular environment, which showing great potential as autonomous robotics for in vivo delivery.
In addition, there are also enzyme@MOF hybrids constructed successfully for their industrial or biotechnological applica
tions. For example, lipase@ZIF-8 for efficient ester hydrolysis [152]; NHase1229@ZIF-67 for efficient nitrile hydration [118]; lipase@Bio-MOF for biodiesel synthesis [119]; individual Cyt c/HRP/CRL/trypsin@Cu-BDC for enhanced activity and stability [120]; laccase@ZIF-8 for de-lignification of biomass [115]; GO supported Cyt c@ZIF-8 for recyclable nano-biocatalysts [153]; Bhb@ZIF-8 for colorimetric biosensing [154]; magnetic \(\mathrm{GOx/Fe_{3}O_{4}@m Z I F-8}\) for colorimetric glucose sensor [155]; GOx@ZIF-8(NiPd) nanoflower for electrochemical glucose biosensors [156]; AuNR-HRP@ZIF-8 for the enhanced bioactivity and sensitivity of enzymatic biosensors [157]; GOx/PVI-Hemin@ZIF-8 for enhanced cascade catalysis [158];
## Multi-enzyme cascades
Stepwise infiltration. Factually, natural processes are always occurred in precise and complicated procedures via synergistic function of multiple enzymes in cascades for chemical transformations, hormonal regulation and so on [159]. The inclusiveness of MOF to various biomacromolecules makes it an unrivaled platform for sophisticated enzyme systems like multi-enzyme cascades by spatially organizing enzymes in confined structures. The products of one enzyme in MOF can yield high local concentration as substrate for another enzyme, thereby enhancing the efficiency of the biocatalytic cascades. The sophisticated problem for multi-enzyme cascades construction is how to well organize each enzyme spatially in MOF scaffolds.
Stepwise infiltration strategy has been demonstrated to be an effective way to reach the maximal encapsulation capacity for all enzymes in multi-enzyme cascades. Zhou et al. systematically compared the enzymatic activity of dual-enzyme tandem nanoreactor GOx/HRP@PCN-888 by two different order of stepwise encapsulation, from large GOx \((6.0\ \mathrm{nm}\ \times\ 5.2\ \mathrm{nm}\ \times\ 7.7\) nm) to small HRP \((4.0\ \mathrm{nm}\ \times\ 4.4\ \mathrm{nm}\ \times\ 6.8\ \mathrm{nm})\) (which term PCN-888-en) or from small HRP to large GOx (which term PCN-888-enR) [96]. PCN-888 afforded three different kind of mesoporous cages including one largest hexacaidecahedron \((6.2\ \mathrm{nm})\) and one medium dodecahedron \((5.0\ \mathrm{nm})\), which are quite fit in well with the molecular dimensions of GOx and HRP respectively (Fig. 7a, b). It was found the GOx/HRP encapsulation with PCN-888-en order showed highest loading capacity for both GOx and HRP, and thereby exhibiting comparable kinetic parameters \((k_{\mathrm{cat}},\,K_{\mathrm{m}}\) and \(\nu_{\mathrm{max}})\) to that of free enzymes (Fig. 7c). However, PCN-888-enR order just showed higher HRP loading capacity whereas the GOx uptake was greatly compromised, because small HRP occupied hexacaidecahedron cage and thus prevented the immobilization of larger GOx.
Based on the aforementioned results, they further reported a robust SC@PCN-333 enzymatic nanofactory by stepwisely infiltrate two tandem enzymes (SOD and CAT) into PCN-333 cavities to protect cells against severe oxidative stress [54]. CAT \((4.9\ \mathrm{nm}\ \times\ 4.4\) \(\mathrm{nm}\times5.6\ \mathrm{nm})\) could fit well in the larger mesoporous hexacaidecahedral cages \((5.5\ \mathrm{nm})\) of cage-type PCN-333 while SOD \((2.8\ \mathrm{nm}\) \(\times\ 3.5\ \mathrm{nm}\ \times\ 4.2\ \mathrm{nm})\) was able to be accommodated in smaller dodecahedral cages \((4.2\ \mathrm{nm})\), and the encapsulation capacity to be maximum \(0.80\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(1.26\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively (Fig. 7d). PCN-333 yielded enhanced local \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) concentration and provided a greenway for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) transportation from SOD to CAT. SC@PCN-333 showed comparable superoxide detoxifying activity and peroxide scavenging activity to that of free enzymes because of the ultrahigh encapsulation capacity for both SOD and CAT, meanwhile exhibited good resistance towards proteases and a broad pH range. What's more, SC@PCN-333 afforded excellent cellular uptake behavior (endocytic and cytosolic) and ultralow cytotoxicity after incubation with HeLa cells, meanwhile, exerted better in cellulo toxic ROS detoxification for up to a week than free CAT/SOD or mixture of individual S@PCN-333 and C@PCN-333 (Fig. 7e). These tandem
nanoreactors showed excellent catalytic performances and longterm stabilities, indicating their potentiality for in vitro and in vivo applications.
In situ co-encapsulation. However, it is impossible for stepwise infiltration strategy to immobilization of more complicated multienzyme cascades, especially for three or more enzymes cascades or for large enzymes. In situ encapsulation approach might be a sensible choice for encapsulation of sophisticated multi-enzyme cascades owing to the merits of inclusiveness and dimension-independent encapsulation to various enzymes. Willner and co-workers constructed four different multi-enzyme cascades through crystallization of ZIF-8 under physiological condition in the presence of enzyme mixtures (Fig. 8a,b) [121]. It was shown that all enzymes were successfully confined in the ZIF-8 frameworks rather than attached to the surface, and also the encapsulated enzymes in ZIF-8 biohybrids could fully retain their intrinsic activities. No matter for two enzymes GOx/HRP@ZIF-8 cascade or for three enzymes \(\beta\)-Gal/GOx/HRP@ZIF-8 cascade, both biocatalytic cascades in the ZIF-8 nanoreactor were around 7.5-fold and 5.3-fold enhancement respectively, when compared with the homogeneous diffusional mixture of enzymes. In addition, this strategy can be employed for the construction of coenzyme-dependent enzyme cascades by further encapsulating coenzyme in ZIF-8 together. It was indicated from \(\mathrm{AlcDH/NAD^{+}@ZIF-8}\) hybrid that electron transfer communication could proceed between \(\mathrm{AlcDH}\) and \(\mathrm{NAD^{+}}\) within ZIF-8. Even for ternary \(\mathrm{AlcDH/NAD^{+}/LacDH@ZIF-8}\) cascade revealed a 4.7-fold enhanced reduction of pyruvic acid to lactic acid compared with the homogeneous enzyme mixtures.
With the clever design of enzyme cascades, the multi-enzyme-encapsulated MOF nanoreactors could have potential applications in biomimetic or biomedical applications. For example, Zhang et al. developed artificial "super neutrophils" by embedding GOx and CPO into ZIF-8 (GOx/CPO@ZIF-8), which could produce hypochlorous acid (HClO) from glucose through enzymatic cascades (Fig. 8c) [123]. The neutrophil membrane-carnouflaged MOFs (GCZM) were able to behavior as a "super neutrophils" to generate large amount of HClO for eradicating tumors and infections in vitro and in vivo. The subcutaneous xenograft 4T1 tumor inhibition indicated the "super neutrophils" exhibited remarkable tumor inhibition effects than the other groups according to the relative tumor volume, weight, H&E staining and TUNEL immunofluorescence analysis (Fig. 8d–f). In addition, Liang and co-works developed a novel chemically-driven micromotor with rapid, reversible pH-responsive motility, via co-encapsulation of succinylated \(\beta\)-Lac and CAT in ZIF-L nanoparticles (\(\beta\)-Lac/CAT@ZIF-L) [124]. \(\beta\)-Lac underwent a reversible gelation process at mild acid condition (5.0), which prevented the access of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) fuel into the micromotors. Furthermore, by enclosing enzyme cascades (GOx and HRP) into MOF capsules with emulsion method, they also constructed a stimulus-responsive artificial cell system, which used to mimic multiple cellular tasks including molecular transport regulation, cell metabolism, communication and programmed degradation [48].
Besides, Ouyang et al. also employed the AAOPE strategy for construction of two-enzyme cascade nanoreactors [112]. The developed GOx/HRP@ZIF-8 resulted in an approximately 3-fold enhancement in the cascade activity when compared with the mixture of free enzymes. Furthermore, this cascade showed a high conversion even after long time storage (15 days), and after heating at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) or exposure to DMF. In addition, some others MOF structures have also been utilized for in situ encapsulation of multi enzymes for tandem reactions. For example, Lv et al. co-immobilized three enzymes (CA, FDH and GDH) sequentially in MOF (GDH/FDH@HKUST-1@CA@MIL-101 (Cr)) via layer-by-layer
assembly for efficient biocatalytic conversion of adsorbed \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) to formate [125].
## Bio-based therapeutics
The intracellular delivery and functionalization of biopharmaceuticals (like peptides, proteins, DNA, RNA, etc.) play critical roles in bio-based therapeutics over the past decades for their treatment of various kind of diseases including cancer, metabolic disorders and autoimmune diseases [160–163]. The efficiency of biopharmaceuticals, however, is limited by their instability, undesired immune response and short half-lifetime. Different from the MOF preservation of enzymes for enzymatic nanofactories, biopharmaceuticals immobilized in MOF hybrids should not only retain their original structures during storage, but also should be released absolutely from the MOF materials under specified condition to perform their biological functions.
## Peptide therapeutics
Peptide, as the fundamental component of proteins, is an important biomolecule for the control of growth, development, immune regulation and metabolism of human body. Peptide drugs have become emerging candidates in disease treatments and scientific research, since their low immunogenicity and deep penetration into cells and tissues. However, some inherent disadvantages like chemical and physical instabilities have hindered their further development. MOF may provide peptides a strong protection against harsh conditions and can also release them quickly on demand. In an early attempt to integrate peptide with MOF, Matsui et al. developed a smart autonomous motors autonomous motor by encapsulating DPA peptide in post-synthesized CuJAST-1 MOF \((0.75\;\mathrm{nm})\) [98]. \(20\ \%\) weight ratio of DPA was successfully loaded within the MOF frameworks. After MOF was partially decomposed by EDTA, DPA peptides were slowly released from the MOF and self-assembly at the interface, which created a large surface-tension gradient to power its translational motion. Such peptide@MOF motors could be further developed for direct the motion toward target location, which demonstrated the great promise in chemical sensing and disease treatment [164].
