Very recently, Liang et al. demonstrated small peptide drugs could also in situ induce the formation of MOFs in mild synthetic conditions [126]. Peptide 46, a peptide drug that can bind the p53 protein and restore the growth suppressor function in cancer cells, was encapsulated into ZIF-8 effectively to retained their intrinsic structures. After being eluted out from ZIF-8 frameworks, their biological activities were fully recovered even exposure to high temperature \((80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , continued freeze-thaw cycles and the proteinase degradation. The encapsulated peptide drugs could be transported and restored under the natural conditions without the aid of cold-chains, which providing an alternative method for the storage of biopharmaceuticals and for in vivo peptide delivery applications.
## Protein therapeutics
The development of biodecomposable MOF makes it feasible for encapsulation and preservation of therapeutic proteins, and
then released out under certain conditions. For example, Farha et al. employed channel-type Zr-based MOF NU-1000 \((3.2\,\mathrm{nm})\) and PCN-222 \((3.7\;\mathrm{nm})\) , which are degradable in the presence of phosphate ions, for encapsulation of insulin \((1.3\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,1.3\ \mathrm{nm}\,\times\,3.4\) nm) via post-synthetic infiltration [99,100]. The robust NU-1000 and PCN-222 could not only allow insulin diffusion into the MOF structures easily with high loading capacity \(34~\%\) and 63 wt. \(\%\) respectively), but also could exclude larger pepsin \((4.5~\times~4.9~\times\) \(6.2\,\mathrm{\nm}^{\mathrm{~\,~}}\) ) mediated digestion (Fig. 9a). Meanwhile, the encapsulated insulin exhibited excellent stability, and the released insulin retained ultrahigh recovered activity \(99\,\%\) and \(84\,\%\) respectively) even being treated with gastric acid or stomach acid \(\mathrm{(pH=1.5\,-}\) 3.5), which demonstrated that NU-1000 and PCN-222 might be utilized as oral carriers of therapeutic insulin for protection against gastric acid corrosion and controlled release (Fig. 9b,c). Importantly, the robust insulin@NU-1000 and insulin@PCN-222 hybrids could facilitate the development of oral insulin medication that transport across the intestinal epithelium into the bloodstream. To enhance the accumulation of MOF biohybrids in target cells, insulin@PCN-222 and insulin@NU-1000 were further functionalized with oligonucleotide sequence, \(5^{\prime}(\mathrm{dGGT})_{10}\) phosphate \(3^{\prime}\,[100]\) . The DNA-functionalized MOF biohybrids (insulin@DNA-NU-1000 or insulin@DNA-PCN-222) not only showed significant colloidal stability in cellular media, but also showed 10-fold enhancement in cellular uptake. Furthermore, insulin@PCN-222 and insulin@NU1000 had no apparent cytotoxicity and antiproliferative effects, and exhibited faster release of insulin in intracellular medium than extracellular medium for the higher inorganic phosphate concentration \((5\mathrm{~-~}10~\mathrm{mM})\) in intracellular fluid than that of serum (1.0 mM). This approach can be generalized to facilitate the delivery of a variety of proteins as biological probes or potential therapeutics.
In situ encapsulation strategy has also exhibited excellent preservation efficiency for different enzymes. It has shown that
In situ encapsulation strategy has also exhibited excellent preservation efficiency for different enzymes. It has shown that biocompatible ZIF frameworks (e.g. ZIF-8, ZIF-10, ZIF-90, and etc.) are able to be decomposed rapidly into pieces in the presence of ATP, EDTA or weak acid conditions \(\left(\mathrm{pH}<6.8\right)\) [52], which might be the excellent platforms for preservation and controlled delivery of therapeutic proteins in vivo. Singamaneni and co-workers preserved insulin with biocompatible ZIF-8 through mixing insulin with ZIF-8 precursors [127]. It was shown that the formation and decomposition of ZIF-8 particles had no bad effect for the biological functionality of insulin. What's more, the optimized insulin@ZIF-8 even showed high preservation efficacy even at elevated temperature, exposure to organic solvent or mechanical agitation (Fig. 9d). Specially, ZIF-8 encapsulated insulin showed more than \(95\,\%\) recovery after one-week storage at 25 or \(40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) whereas only \(50~\%\) retained for free insulin. It was also found the secondary structure of insulin spontaneously changed from \(\alpha\) -helical to \(\upbeta\) -sheet under harsh conditions, while encapsulated insulin within ZIF8 was able to maintain the intrinsic structure of pristine insulin (Fig. 9e). In addition, Antibody is another kind of biopharmaceuticals that have been widely applied in scientific research, medical diagnosis, and disease treatment. It was found the MOF preserved antibody (antibody@MOF) exhibited extraordinary thermal, chemical, and long-term stabilities, and could be completely released out on demand [128]. After pre-treatment, the released antibody exhibited comparable binding capacity to that of the untreated one, which may greatly broaden the application of protein pharmaceuticals in resource-limited settings or underserved populations/regions.
Inspired by the aforementioned work, MOFs have also been successfully employed as preservation and intracellular delivery platforms for therapeutic proteins in the fields of genome editing, cancer therapy, immunotherapy and others. Wang's and Mao's groups employed ATP responsive ZIF-90 as a general platform for cytosolic protein delivery and CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing [129].
Protein@ZIF-90 hybrids could be disintegrated by ATP in cytosol \((1\mathrm{~-~}10\mathrm{~mM})\) and deliver a large variety of proteins (e.g. BSA, SOD, GFP, RNase A, Cas9 nuclease) inside cells, while it was not in the extracellular environment \(\mathrm{^{\prime}A T P<0.4\ m M)}\) . The delivery of cytotoxic RNase A from RNase \(\mathsf{A@Z I F-90}\) showed a significant decrease of viability ( \(15\,\%\) versus \(85\,\%\) for free RNase at \(135\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) ) for HeLa cells, emphasizing the high efficiency of ZIF-90 to deliver cytotoxic proteins for cancer therapy. Meanwhile, ZIF-90 was also an effective platform for Cas9 nuclear delivery in genome editing. It was shown that the Cas9@ZIF-90 hybrids could efficiently knock out the GFP expression up to \(40\,\%\) in HeLa cells compare to untreated HeLa cells.
Several surface modification strategies were also employed for enhanced dispersion and accumulation in vivo. Chu et al. modi
Several surface modification strategies were also employed for enhanced dispersion and accumulation in vivo. Chu et al. modified protein@ZIF-8 with biocompatible PVP coatings, which showed well dispersion, rapid cellular uptake, excellent protein recovery, long-term stability, and efficient intracellular delivery of individual proteins (e.g. EGFP, \(\upbeta\) -Gal, Anti-tubulin Ab) and multiple-protein systems (e.g. Caspase 3/HAS, BSA/RFP/ \(\upbeta\) -Gal) [130]. Ren and Qu et al. developed a MOF-based vaccine by in situ encapsulating OVA antigen within ZIF-8 and further absorbing vaccine adjuvants CpG ODNs for enhanced systemic immune and memory response [131]. With the recognition of CpG ODNs by mammalian immune system (TLR9), the OVA@ZIF-8-CpG composites caused nearly 3-fold higher TNF- \(\upalpha\) secretion and 2.5-fold higher TNF- \(\upgamma\) secretion than that elicited by individual mixtures, which indicated the enhanced immunostimulatory activity.
In addition, Zheng et al. developed an EV membrane-modified gelonin@ZIF-8 (EMP) for preservation and intracellular delivery of cancer therapeutic gelonin [132]. Biocompatible ZIF-8 particles could protect gelonin against protease digestion and evade phagocytosis, meanwhile the camouflaged EV membrane could help EMP nanoparticles to selectively target on homotypic tumour sites and therefore promoting cellular uptake (Fig. 9f). EMP could be effectively internalized by homotypic cells through the endocytosis pathway with 2- to 8-fold higher than others non-homotypic cells. Meanwhile, the intracellular top OH microenvironment at tumour sites would lead to in situ degradation of ZIF-8 particles to release encapsulated gelonin protein. In vitro and in vivo studies indicated the camouflaged EMP nanoparticles showed longer retention time and accumulation efficiency. Also, EMP nanoparticles specifically accumulated at tumour site with about 11- and 4-fold higher than those of free gelonin and gelonin@ZIF-8 (MP) respectively, and the tumour cell growth was significantly inhibited in vivo (Fig. 9g). All these results indicated MOFs have showed their great talents as universal biomimetic platforms for intracellular delivery of therapeutic proteins with high efficacy, which is potentially applicable for biomedical or biotechnological applications.
## Gene-based therapeutics
MOF strategy also shows great promise for preservation and intracellular delivery of genetic molecules in gene-based theranostics. Due to the charge property and dimensional matching, oligonucleotides can be readily encapsulated into the MOF frameworks via post-synthetic infiltration to form the bio@MOF composites, which are often used for nucleic acid detection or gene delivery. Lin group reported the UiO MOFs for co-deliveryof cisplatin prodrug and pooled siRNAs to enhance therapeutic efficacy against ovarian cancer cells [165]. Fairon-Jimenez and co-workers employed NU-1000 to load, protect, and deliver siRNAs without the need for chemical modifications [101]. It was found that MOF could protect internalized siRNA from nuclease degradation, enhance cellular uptake, and promote siRNA escape from endosomes to silence MDR genes and others cancer-causing genes, which might applicable for future gene therapies.
Very recently, Deng and Zhou group developed a series of channel-type MOFs from NiRMOF-74-II to -V with progressively tuned pore size to achieve the precise inclusion of ssDNA (Fig. 10a) [102]. The entire ssDNA chain could completely confine inside the pores of all MOF series to protect the payload from degradation. Because the interaction strength and pore geometry dictate the kinetics of uptake, protection and release of ssDNA, the small Ni-IRMOF-74-II showed the best release performance over other Ni-IRMOF-74 series. Intracellular delivery assay of ssDNA indicated that both Ni-IRMOF-74-II and -III vectors with weaker interactions presented excellent transfection efficacy in different mammalian immune cells including primary mouse immune cells \(\mathrm{CD4+T}\) cell and B cells) and macrophages (RAW264.7 from mouse and THP-1 from human), which were unrivaled by the commercialized agents.
The aforementioned oligonucleotides-MOF composites exhibited better structural regularity and excellent transfection efficacy to cells, but the metal nodes and organic ligands used have been proved high cytotoxicity and poor biological metabolites, which was not appliable for in vivo application. In situ encapsulation of oligonucleotides, analogous to that of enzymes, might be an effective method to revise such difficulties. MOF precursors could be nucleated around the nucleic acids to form robust coatings for gene protection and intracellular delivery. Shukla's group achieved the in situ encapsulation of exogenous plasmid DNA (dsDNA) in ZIF8 via biomimetic mineralization [133]. The encapsulated plasmid retained its functional activity while transfected mammalian cancer cells for gene expression.
Almost at the same time, Tang's group employed the same strategy to encapsulate large plasmid DNA (pEGFP-C1, 4700 bp) in ZIF-8 via biomimetic mineralization (pEGFP-C1@ZIF-8) and coprecipitation (pEGFP-C1@ZIF-8-PEI 25KD) for intracellular gene delivery and expression (Fig. 10b) [134]. The embeded pDNA in both systems were well-distributed throughout the ZIF-8 nanostructures and benefited from effective protection against the DNase I degradation. Impressively, It was found that both ZIF-8 vectors could efficiently transfect MCF-7 cells. The pDNA@ZIF-8 composites coprecipitated with high molecular PEI-25 kD reached higher transfection efficacy than that of biomimetic mineralization, since PEI-25 kD polymer enhanced the electrostatic binding of pDNA for better cellular uptake and endo-/lysosomal escape in gene expression (Fig. 10c). The encapsulation of oligonucleotides within MOFs, either through post-synthetic infiltration or in situ encapsulation, exhibited enhanced loading capacity, better intracellular delivery, and stronger binding affinity, which might be promising nonviral vectors for gene-based therapies, such as RNA silencing, DNA/RNA vaccines, immunotoxin treatment and so on.
## Clinical biofluids
The availability of high-quality bodily fluids such as blood, urine, sputum, secretion, etc. is essential in the early diagnosis and therapeutic intervention of diseases because they are rich in disease-related biomarkers [166,167]. They are inevitably relied on the "cold chain" for shipping, handling as well as storage because of their fragile structures, which is an insurmountable obstacle in resource-limited settings (no electricity, refrigeration, and trained personnel). A feasible solution is developing a universal refrigeration-free preservation strategy that all biomarkers can be preserved to retain their own biological functionalities without refrigerator during handling, transport and storage.
Different from the enzymatic nanofactories and protein therapeutics, there are numerous biomarkers, hormones, nutrients, mineral salts and cells in bodily fluids to block MOF nucleation, which greatly increased the difficulties for encapsulation and preservation within MOFs. In situ encapsulation approach provides a feasible way to fully immobilize numerous protein biomarkers simultaneously within MOF structures for their inclusiveness to different biomacromolecules. Ouyang et al. have made an attempt to encapsulate human serum with ZIF-8 using the AAOPE strategy, and found whole-spectrum biomarkers in serum were fully encapsulated into ZIF-8 nanochambers with excellent stability and ultra-high recovery [112].
Singamaneni et al. introduced a universal biospecimen preservation technique via ZIF-8 in situ encapsulation for preservation of whole protein biomarkers in bodily fluids including urine, serum, plasma or blood [53]. The ZIF-8 encapsulated biospecimen were further air-dried onto a paper substrate to overcome the drawbacks of conventional dry spot technology (e.g. limited stability, elution efficiency and inaccurate concentration), which could handling, transport, and storage under normal conditions (Fig. 11a). SDS-PAGE showed whole protein biomarkers in biofluids could be fully encapsulated into ZIF-8 nanoparticles, and could be eluted out quickly under weak acid condition with small amount of EDTA. Taking urinary NGAL (for acute kidney injury) and serum/plasma CA-125 (ovarian cancer) as model biomarkers, ZIF-8 showed excellent preservation efficacy for biomarkers in spiked artificial biofluids even treated with elevated temperature for long time, which was comparable with the freeze – thawed liquid sample (Fig. 11b, c). After evaluating three patient urine samples with acute kidney injury, ZIF-8 encapsulation showed more than 85 % NGAL recovery after storage for 1 week at 40 °C, whereas only less than 40 % NGAL was recovered without ZIF-8 encapsulation. Moreover, this technique could simultaneously encapsulate multiple acute kidney
injury biomarkers (albumin, NGAL, \(\beta\)-2 microglobulin, and cystatin C) with fully preservation efficacy (\(>95\%\)). Homoplastically, CA-125 from ZIF-8 encapsulated serum, heparin-plasma and EDTA-plasma exhibited more than \(80\,\%\) recovery after 4 weeks storage at \(40\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), but only \(40\,\%\) or less recovery for those without ZIF-8 encapsulation. More than \(90\,\%\) preservation efficacy was also observed after 10 days of unknown shipping and handling conditions. This technique would enable patients to self-prepare preserved samples easily and send to hospitals or clinical laboratories via regular mailing.
## Preservation of viruses and living organisms
MOF technique has been demonstrated great success for preservation of biomolecules and biomacromolecules. It might be a promising metrics for more complicated bioentities, such as organelles, viruses, microorganisms, differentiated cells, and even biological organs [42,55]. MOF has shown its great potential as protective exoskeletons via biomimetic mineralization around prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells, which not only affords prolonged protection against external environment stress, but also permits
the transport of nutrients or chemical stimulants necessary for viability while preventing access of larger cytotoxic components. Meanwhile, the preserved organisms are able to be alive to resume vegetative growth and function after MOF coating being eluted under certain conditions.
## Viruses
Viruses are considered to be biological entities that depend entirely on their hosts for their replication. However, they are usually excluded from living organisms since lack the functional autonomy, metabolic activities and the reproductive behavior inherent in life [168,169]. Despite the huge amount of controversy, viruses are still classified into the non-living organisms in this review and described in this section. Sometimes, viruses can cause the unwanted incident of infectious diseases by attacking the host subjects, but it is still ubiquitous in the field of vaccines, gene/drug vectors, cellular fusion, and so on [170]. Biomimetic mineralization with MOF can not only enhance the structural stability and biological functionality of virus under denaturing environmental stressors, but also be used as template to regulate the size and shape of multifarious MOF materials with the anisotropic viral capsids [55].
Gassensmith et al. have made indelible contributions to the biomimetic mineralization of MOF on TMV virus by mixed MOF precursors together with target virus under conditions favor the framework formation [135,136]. It was shown that robust rod-shaped TMV@ZIF-8 core-shell hybrids with good uniformity were
Gassensmith et al. have made indelible contributions to the biomimetic mineralization of MOF on TMV virus by mixed MOF precursors together with target virus under conditions favor the framework formation [135,136]. It was shown that robust rod-shaped TMV@ZIF-8 core-shell hybrids with good uniformity were formed from the coordination of Hmim and \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) in the presence of TMV template, and the thickness of ZIF-8 shells could be tuned by changing the precursor ratio or concentrations (Fig. 12a-c). Interestingly, both TMV@ZIF-8 with thin and thick shells fared well in polar solvents (methanol and DMF), and even endure immersion in nonpolar DCM or boiling water without obvious structural degradation. Due to the porosity of ZIF-8 shell that allowed the diffusion of small substrates, some bioconjugation reactions could also take place from the functional residues of exterior TMV surface even in ZIF-8 encapsulation state. Herein, TMV@ZIF-8 composites were successfully used to perform classic diazonium coupling reaction and \(\mathrm{{Cu^{I}}}\) -catalyzed azide – alkyne cycloaddition reaction by functionalizing the TMV surface-exposed tyrosine residues with functional linkers (Fig. 12d, e) [136]. Encapsulation of virus into MOF structures may provide a new avenue for drug/gene delivery, immunogenic response, and bioengineering.
Furthermore, the same group also constructed a MOF preserved viral vaccine to improve the stability and immunogenic response in vivo by encapsulating living TMV virus into ZIF-8 [137]. The encapsulated TMV versus naked TMV showed excellent stability after stressing under different harsh conditions including methanol, elevated temperature \((100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , 6 M guanidinium chloride and ethyl acetate. Importantly, the ZIF-8 encapsulation and exfoliation process would not damage the viral RNA, and the encapsulated TMV virus showed comparable infection capability with native TMV for Nicotiana benthamiana plants. In vivo experiments in BALB/c mice indicated TMV@ZIF-8 composites elicited a production of anti-TMV antibody that significantly higher than naked TMV after
35 days immunogenic response. Furthermore, TMV@ZIF-8 showed high retention time in vivo and no observable damage to the skin or vital organs, which indicated a good strategy for the preservation of virus, and fabricating advanced hybrid materials for virus biotechnologies.
## Living organisms
The individual encapsulation and preservation of living cells, such as fungi, bacteria and mammalian cells, has great impact on the area of fermentation engineering, cell biology as well as clinical applications [171], which promise the cytoprotection of encased cells against lethal stressors [7]. Compared with others bioinspired protective coatings reported previously, including silica [172], silica-italia [173], graphene [174], polydopamine [15], and \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\)-TA coordination complexes [25], MOFs have been demonstrated to be promising candidates as cytoprotective shells due to their chemical robustness, biocompatibility, degradability, and semi-permeability to nutrients or chemical stimulants [138].
## Living fungi
Falcans and co-workers have made pioneering achievements in biomimetic mineralization of ZIF-8 as cytoprotective exoskeletons for preserving living fungi Saccharomyces cerevisiae (baker's yeast, eukaryotic) [138]. The encapsulation of individual living cells within ZIF-8 was carried out by dispersing living cells in an aqueous solution containing an appropriate amount of Hmlm and \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) precursors. ZIF-8 formed a selectively permeable exoskeleton on living cells with the thickness being precisely controlled in the \(100{-}250\ \mathrm{nm}\) range through sequential coating steps (Fig. 13a-c). Cell reproduction profiles indicated ZIF-8 coated yeast (yeast@ZIF8) survived for more than \(95\ \%\) after being cultured in lyticase presented cell media for \(^{3\mathrm{~h~}}\), while only \(5\%\) remained for native yeast. Interestingly, the MOF coatings could remarkably prevent the division of yeast cells and thereby extended cell lifetime by inducing an artificial hibernation state, reminiscent of the "sporulation
process" in nature. After the removal of MOF shells, the yeast cells could quickly turn to the "germination process" with exponential growth to a similar level of native yeast (Fig. 13d). Remarkably, MOFs could form a protective coating for controlling the transportation of nutritive substances to the cell cytoplasm meanwhile preserving cell life, which might be potential candidate materials for artificial cell exoskeletons.
Not only that, the bioengineered MOF coating could also preserve the cell life even in an oligotrophic environment. \(\upbeta\) -Gal was first coated on yeast cells via electrostatic interactions for production of the nutrient glucose from lactose in situ. The ZIF-8 coatings were further formed on \(\upbeta\) -Gal immobilized yeast cells (yeast \(\mathbb{\left/\beta\right.}\) - gal/ZIF-8) via biomimetic mineralization process, which could not only provide the conversion and transportation of lactose to yeast cells, but also inhibited the degradation of \(\upbeta\) -Gal and yeast cells (Fig. 13e) [50]. The immobilized yeast survived for more than \(70\,\%\) even after 7 days in an oligotrophic lactose-based media even in the presence of lyticase or protease, while only \(5\%\) viability for naked yeast cells (Fig. 13f). It was demonstrated that MOF coatings could not only protect both \(\upbeta\) -gal enzyme and cells against damage from proteolytic condition, acidic environment as well as UV attack, but also enabled the survival of yeast cells in an oligotrophic environment for a long time. Moreover, the yeast cells regained their full growth potential following the removal of the ZIF-8 exoskeletons (Fig. 13g).
## Living bacteria
Furthermore, the biomimetic mineralization of living cells showed extraordinary promise in technological applications. Escherichia coli (E. coli, bacterium) has been shown a tumor-colonizing bacteria, which makes it applicable for tumor-targeted therapeutic delivery, tumor microenvironment modulation, bioimaging and so on [139]. It was found that the ZIF-8 coating could preserve the viability and tumor selectivity of gram-negative E. coli. After co-loaded with photosensitizer (Ce6) and antitumor drug (DOX) through one-pot immobilization, the E. coli@ZIF-8/C&D
biocomposites could deliver DOX and Ce6 to tumor sites through E. coli-targeted drug delivery and exhibited excellent therapeutic efficacy both in vitro and in vivo under mild near-infrared light irradiation. In addition, Peng et al. coated E coli. with MOF through in situ assembly to fabricate N, P co-doped porous carbon capsules, which leading to remarkable electrochemical properties for advanced energy storage in lithium–sulfur batteries and zinc–air batteries [140].
MOF coating could also preserve the anaerobic bacteria to survive in \(O_{2}\) stress while maintaining the biological metabolism. Yang's group cooperated with Yaghi's group for cytoprotection of anaerobic bacteria Morella thermoacetica in photosynthesis through uniformly wrapping with a post-synthetic Zr-BTB MOF monolayer (Fig. 13h) [141]. It was observed that MOF coated Morella
thermoacetica showed nearly \(80\%\) decrease in death after being incubated in air for 2 days compared to the bear Morella thermoacetica, which was comparable with those cultured under anaerobic conditions. Additionally, the Zr-BTB coating could also decompose reactive oxygen species (ROS) by Zr clusters to prevent the ROS accumulation and thereby elongated the lifetime in oxidative stress. Moreover, it was found that the MOF wrapped bacteria maintained 2-fold increase in the photosynthesis productivity of acetate from \(CO_{2}\) fixation under oxidative stress (Fig. 13i). Meanwhile, Zr-BTB MOF was further successfully employed for the encapsulation of gram-negative bacteria E. coli. All these achievements indicated that MOF might be a promising cytoprotective material for future preservation of sex cells, embryonic stem cells and even mammalian organs.
## Preservation of biological devices
Apart from the aforementioned biomacromolecules and living organisms, MOFs also exhibit great promise for the encapsulation and preservation of biotechnological devices including protein patterns, biodiagnostic devices as well as biochips, which have exhibited great potential application in modern biomedical technologies [175–178]. MOFs were formed on the biological structures as protective coatings via one-pot encapsulation for retaining the construction or preserving activities of biocomponents.
Liang and Falcaro found MOF not only can encapsulate the biomacromolecules dispersed in aqueous solutions, but also can encapsulate their patterns on solid matrixes via biomimetic mineralization approach (Fig. 14a) [142]. It was indicated that both ZIF-8 and Ln-MOF could biomimetically replicated on the stamped BSA pattern even size less than \(5~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) after transferring with aqueous solution of MOF precursors (Hmlm and \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) for ZIF-8, \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}/\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}\) and \(\mathtt{B D C}^{2-}\) for Ln-MOF). Moreover, this biomimetic replication technique could be used for the preservation and replication of fingerprints for identifying individuals at crime scenes, which including proteins, peptides, fatty acids and inorganic salts (Fig. 14b). The replication of fingerprints with Ln-MOF displayed strong luminescence from the fingerprint patterns on various substrates including plastic, metal, and glass, which potentially aids in crime scene investigation. Importantly, the MOF replicated fingerprints could preserve their information for a long time without any loss of the friction ridge details, demonstrating the versatility of this technique for cold case analysis.
