Antibody biorecognition plays essential roles for numerous biomedical devices such as biomedical diagnostic chips, targeted drug delivery, targeted cancer imaging, and so on. [179]. However, it limits the extensive fabrication and application because of the stability issues when stored under nonrefrigeration conditions. Therefore, it is of paramount importance to enhancing the stability of antibodies on matrixes under ambient and elevated temperatures for many nanobiotechnological applications. Wang et al. measured the biorecognition capability of the anti-CD-146 monoclonal antibody at the single-molecule level through AFM-based force spectroscopy (Fig. 14c) [143]. After forming ZIF-8 coating on the anti-CD-146 antibody functionalized AFM tip, it was found that
the functionalized AFM tip showed nearly unchanged recognition capabilities (at 112, 210, and \(324~\mathsf{p N}\)) compared with fresh tip in probing the CD-146 antigen on the surface of melanoma cell after 3 days of storage at \(50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), in contrast to a complete loss of recognition capability of the antibodies without the protective coating (Fig. 14d). Not only that, the MOF coating also showed better preservation efficacy than commonly used sucrose coating in terms of maintaining the binding force and frequency of the antibody to antigen (Fig. 14e). Considering antibody-antigen recognition forms the basis of various nanobiotechnologies based on antibody-conjugated nanostructures, we believe this MOF technique is potentially applicable for preservation of a variety of biological devices for biomedical or biotechnological applications.
Furthermore, Singaporean et al. constructed a plasmonic diagnostic biochip by immobilizing IgG antibody-conjugated AuNR (AuNR-IgG) on glass or paper substrate and demonstrated that ZIF-8 coatings were highly effective in preserving its biorecognition capability to anti-IgG (Fig. 14f) [144]. The plasmonic biochip showed good relationship between the LSPR shift and anti-IgG concentration in the range of \(240\,\mathrm{pg\,mL^{-1}}\) and \(24\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\). It is shown that a thin ZIF-8 coating was successfully formed on AuNR-IgG conjugates with nearly \(30\,\mathrm{nm}\) LSPR red shift, while totally removed by rinsing with distilled water at \(\mathsf{p H}\,6\). Moreover, with the preservation of ZIF-8, AuNR-IgG biochip recovered more than \(70\ \%\) of recognition capability to anti-IgG after one-week storage at room temperature, 40 and \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), whereas over \(90\;\%\) lost within the first day for naked biochip (Fig. 14g). Not only that, it showed better preserving efficacy than traditional used polymers like silk. After optimizing the formation parameters of ZIF-8, the coated AuNR-IgG biochips also showed excellent recognition capability under several harsh conditions including elevated temperature \((80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\), DMF, and protease solution [51]. In addition, MOF coating could also be used to preserve the paper-based biochips and exhibited quite similar efficacy. The finding helps the discovery of MOF technique to potentially applicable for various kind of biomedical test papers.
## Conclusion and future prospects
Research focused on the interfaces of MOF encapsulated bioentities has moved toward the exploration of novel biocomposites that have demonstrated great promise in the fields of biotechnological and biomedical applications. Insight into the interfacial chemistry and structure geometry, we first elaborate the synthetic strategies to encapsulate bioentities into MOF structures with enhanced performance. Then, recent efforts on the preservation of different fragile biological entities from biomacromolecules, viruses and living organisms to biological devices are comprehensively summarized in this review for improving stability, catalytic performance and biological behavior. We have highlighted the preservation capability of MOFs to different bioentities for the application in enzymatic nanofactories, enzymen cascades, delivery of biopharmaceuticals and stabilization of organisms and biological devices. Many remarkable bioentity@MOF composites were developed, not only for enhancement of the stability and bioactivity of entrapped bioentities, but also for the design of novel bio-functional materials.
Although great success, this research field is still in its preliminary stage and rapidly evolving. There are still many challenges that need to be addressed before MOF platform can reach its full potential. MOF precursors can induce an irreversible denaturation and inactivation of bioentities to some extent, makes it difficult to expand for fragile biosystems. The further exploration of basic problems like the MOF chemistry, the formation of biointerfaces and the orientation and distribution of bioentity, is still indispens
able for the discovery of robust biological MOFs with excellent encapsulation efficacy and preserved bioactivities. In addition, it will provide new possibilities for MOF composites to further integrate smart functions that decomposed in response to external stimuli, such as pH, enzymes, buffers and others. Few MOFs including ZIF series and NU series have been employed as delivery platforms for controlled release of biomarkers or biothepeutics. The long-term stability under physiological condition and smart responsibility to target location of MOF biocomposites are needed to be further evaluated for their potential application in vivo.
Furthermore, more and more attentions have been focused on the interfaces of MOF-organism or MOF-micro/nano biotechnology platforms in recent years for their promise in microbial engineering, clinical medicine and others. Prior to these MOF encapsulation strategies being translated into macroscopic things, the new MOF-bio interfaces, growth mechanisms of MOF and long-term effects to entities need to be evaluated. Such knowledge gaps may make new research breakthroughs of novel biotechnologies, and will underpin exploration to sophisticated biosystems that biologically vulnerable under real implementation settings, including microbial engineering, food security, stem-cell therapy as well as organ transplantation.
MOFs are becoming omnipotent platforms for encapsulating and preserving various type of biological entities from biomacromolecules, living organisms to biodevices. Accordingly, this technique has demonstrated its great success in enzymatic production, biopresservation and biobanking without "cold-chains". We anticipate that capitalizing on the rational design of bioentity@MOF composites will be rewarding in "technological innovations" to solve new challenges in biotechnological and biomedical applications.
